/*!
\page building Building

There are several steps to build the project for your platform, firstly you need to \ref
cloning "clone the repository" itself, then you need to gather all required dependencies, and
finally you can \ref makefile "build the project" as well as \ref documentation "documentation"
locally!

\tableofcontents

\section building_cloning Cloning

Firstly, there are some basic dependencies required by the project, such as:
 - git
 - cmake
 - make
 - binutils
 - gcc or a modern c compiler with stdc17 support
 - cross-compiling tools (_optional, only if you want to contribute or test_)
 - doxygen (_optional, only if you require documentation_)
 - various other dependencies (_again optional, only if you want to contribute or test_)

Fortunately, many of these dependencies are already readily available on most linux distributions,
but, if you do not have them already downloaded they can be installed with:

```bash
sudo ./get-deps
```

Note that using the command above will install all dependencies, including the ones that are
considered optional for contribution and testing of the repository. You can then clone the
repository as well as submodules with:

```bash
git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/Codeca-Labs/tapi.git
```

If you have already cloned the repository and are having trouble with submodules, or you have
mistakenly cloned the repository without the ```--recurse-submodules``` flag, you can fix this by running:

```bash
git submodule update --init --recursive
```

\section building_makefile Library building/ Makefile

This project uses both make and cmake(_in order to build documentation_) to build the entire library
. The makefile is located in the root of the repository, and has various targets that can be used
for various purposes. If you are not familiar with makefiles, and are simply interested in building
the library, it can be built with:

```bash
sudo make install
```

Which will build the library for your native architecture/ platform, and then install it to the
default location (_not currently supported or configurable for windows_).

We provide a list of all available targets in the makefile, some of which are:
 - ```make install```, which will build the library for your native architecture/ platform
 - ```make uninstall```, which will remove the library from the default location
 - ```make clean```, which will remove all build artifacts and temporary files
 - ```make test_native```, which will build the library for your native architecture/ platform as
 well as all tests
 - ```make test_all_arch```, which will build the library for all supported architectures and
 platforms, including all tests (_requires optional dependencies_)

All of these targets can be run from the root of the repository. If you are purely interested in
contributing to the project with newer tests, then you can directly build the tests with the
makefile within the ```tests``` directory. Some of the targets that can be used for the various
types of tests are:
 - ```make all```, which will build all tests
 - ```make run```, which will run all tests
 - ```make clean```, which will remove all build artifacts and temporary files

These targets are often used by the makefile within the root project directory and are rarely used
for individual tests. Running each of the tests individually will only require qemu and optional
dependencies if you are testing on a different architecture/ platform. For further safety when
running each test you can set ```use_asan=true``` to enable the linking of your default compilers
address sanitizer for checking of memory leaks and other memory safety issues (_requires optional
dependencies_).

\section building_documentation Documentation

Like stated above, this project uses doxygen as well as doxygen-awesome-css(_these require you to
install doxygen through your pm, this will soon be added to get-deps_) to generate this
documentation you are reading right now. The documentation can be generated locally with:

```bash
./get-docs

# or better yet...
cmake --build build --target doc
```

Both of these commands will generate doxygen documentation in the ```build/doc``` directory which
can be opened with your browser of choice:

```bash
xdg-open ./build/doc/html/index.html
```
*/